    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-14545",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013,$ find $\\sigma^2.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X\\ge 3) \\\\  & =1-0.4013 \\\\  & =0.5987\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.5987 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5987)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.5987) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.25 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
